  array (
    'type' => 'resource',
    'tab' => 34,
    'meta_title' => 'Lake Allatoona High Water: Protecting Acworth Basements',
    'meta_description' => 'When Lake Allatoona runs high, here\'s how Acworth lake homes protect basements. Water inside? Call (470) 672-2095.',
    'eyebrow' => 'LAKE ALLATOONA FLOODING • ACWORTH, GEORGIA',
    'h1' => 'Lake Allatoona High Water: A Basement Checklist for Acworth Homes',
    'intro' => 
    array (
      'text' => 'Acworth calls itself "The Lake City" for good reason, but living near Lake Allatoona and its creeks means paying attention when the water rises. The lake is managed by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, and after long stretches of heavy rain it can sit far above normal for weeks. Here\'s what has happened before, how to track the lake and creeks, and how to protect your basement when the water comes up.',
      'links' => 
      array (
      ),
    ),
    'buttons' => 
    array (
      'b1' => 'Call (470) 672-2095',
      'b2' => 'Request Emergency Help',
    ),
    'blocks' => 
    array (
      0 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'h2',
        'text' => 'How high can Lake Allatoona get?',
      ),
      1 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'para',
        'text' => 'After prolonged heavy rain, Lake Allatoona has risen more than 18 feet above its normal level, flooding roads like Tanyard Creek Road near Acworth.',
        'links' => 
        array (
        ),
      ),
      2 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'para',
        'text' => 'Allatoona Lake was authorized by the Flood Control Acts of 1941 and 1946, and Allatoona Dam on the Etowah River was completed in 1949. One of its jobs is storing floodwater, which means that after big storms the Corps holds water back in the lake to protect communities downstream. WSB-TV reported one event where the lake sat about 11 feet above normal at 851 feet, and later more than 18 feet higher than normal, with Tanyard Creek Road, a main route from Acworth to I-75, underwater and Old Stilesboro Road shut down over concerns about Allatoona Creek.',
        'links' => 
        array (
        ),
      ),
      3 => 
