  '/resources/musty-basement-water-damage-signs' => 
  array (
    'type' => 'resource',
    'tab' => 35,
    'meta_title' => 'Musty Basement Smell: 7 Hidden Water Damage Signs',
    'meta_description' => 'Musty finished basement in Acworth? 7 signs of hidden water damage and what to do next. Call (470) 672-2095.',
    'eyebrow' => 'MUSTY BASEMENT SMELL • ACWORTH, GEORGIA',
    'h1' => 'Musty Basement Smell? 7 Hidden Water Damage Signs to Check',
    'intro' => 
    array (
      'text' => 'You finished the basement, the AC is running and it still smells musty. That\'s one of the most common complaints from Acworth homeowners, and it almost always means moisture is getting in somewhere. Here are seven signs that point to hidden water damage, why basements around Acworth are so prone to it, and what to do before a smell turns into a mold problem.',
      'links' => 
      array (
      ),
    ),
    'buttons' => 
    array (
      'b1' => 'Call (470) 672-2095',
      'b2' => 'Request Help Online',
    ),
    'blocks' => 
    array (
      0 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'h2',
        'text' => 'What causes a musty smell in a basement?',
      ),
      1 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'para',
        'text' => 'A musty basement smell comes from mold or mildew growing on damp materials, usually because water is seeping in or humidity is staying too high.',
        'links' => 
        array (
        ),
      ),
      2 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'para',
        'text' => 'That earthy odor is made by gases mold gives off as it grows. Mold needs moisture, and basements get it from three places: water coming through the walls or floor, leaks from plumbing and appliances, and humid air that condenses on cool surfaces. In Acworth, where many homes are built into clay hillsides and summers are humid, all three show up often.',
        'links' => 
        array (
        ),
      ),
      3 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'h3',
        'text' => 'Quick Answers',
      ),
      4 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'defs',
        'items' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'What the smell means',
            'text' => 'mold or mildew growing on something damp',
            'links' => 
            array (
            ),
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Most common causes',
            'text' => 'groundwater seepage, plumbing leaks, high humidity',
            'links' => 
            array (
            ),
          ),
          2 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Ideal indoor humidity',
            'text' => 'about 30% to 50%',
            'links' => 
            array (
            ),
          ),
          3 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Biggest hiding spots',
            'text' => 'behind drywall, under carpet pad, along the wall-floor joint',
            'links' => 
            array (
            ),
          ),
          4 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'When to worry',
            'text' => 'a smell that lasts more than a few days or returns after rain',
            'links' => 
            array (
            ),
          ),
          5 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'First step',
            'text' => 'find the moisture source, not just the smell',
            'links' => 
            array (
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      5 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'h2',
        'text' => 'The 7 hidden signs of water damage in a basement',
      ),
      6 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'h3',
        'text' => '1. The smell gets stronger after rain',
      ),
      7 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'para',
        'text' => 'If the odor spikes a day or two after a storm, groundwater is likely getting in through the walls or floor.',
        'links' => 
        array (
        ),
      ),
      8 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'h3',
        'text' => '2. White, chalky residue on block walls',
      ),
      9 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'para',
        'text' => 'That powder is efflorescence: minerals left behind when water moves through concrete or block and evaporates. It\'s proof water is passing through the wall.',
        'links' => 
        array (
        ),
      ),
      10 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'h3',
        'text' => '3. Swollen or separating baseboards',
      ),
      11 => 
      array (
        'kind' => 'para',
        'text' => 'Baseboards along exterior walls that swell, crack or pull away often mean the bottom of the drywall behind them is wet.',
        'links' => 
        array (
        ),
      ),
      12 => 
